WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.450
Never before has Pixar taken so long to make
a sequel as they did with Incredibles 2, but

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.220
the 14-year wait was well worth it.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.080
The movie is jaw-droppingly gorgeous, with
style to spare and action sequences so tense,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.999
you forget that nothing on the screen is actually
real.

00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:20.279
With Incredibles 2 clocking in as the longest
Pixar movie to date, you can't say you didn't

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:21.279
get your money's worth.

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:26.031
Also, Pixar has plugged a mind-boggling number
of Easter eggs and references into the movie

00:00:26.031 --> 00:00:28.820
to reward sharp eyes and repeat viewings.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.779
It's all but impossible to catch them all
the first time around.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:35.341
Whether you've seen the movie and are wondering
what you missed, or are going to the movie

00:00:35.341 --> 00:00:39.520
and want to be prepared, here are the Easter
eggs we caught in Incredibles 2.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.990
And of course, spoilers ahead.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.340
Baby Mozart

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.840
Edna Mode and Jack Jack Parr are the best
team-up Incredibles 2 has to offer.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.510
Edna, the icy inventor and costume designer,
is completely won over by the little superhero

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.720
when she sees the breadth of his powers, and
the duo end up spending hours together while

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.760
Mr. Incredible gets some rest.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.840
When Bob returns to pick up Jack Jack, Edna
mentions that the boy has taken a liking to

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.230
the music of Mozart.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.770
It's not a random reference, when it comes
to classical composers, Jack Jack has an established

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:10.799
preference.

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:15.880
In the 2005 short film Jack Jack Attack, Jack
Jack's babysitter sets up a number of neurologically

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.460
stimulating activities for the baby, and the
selection includes a Mozart CD.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.289
Turns out, the music really does bring something
out in Jack Jack, inspiring him to make the

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:28.130
babysitter really earn her pay by destroying
the entire house.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.799
There's clearly something to Mozart's music
that brings out the monster in the little

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:35.479
man, and naturally, Edna loves that.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:36.749
Smelling Ratz

00:01:36.749 --> 00:01:41.729
One of Pixar's longest-running Easter eggs
involves actor John Ratzenberger, whose voice

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:44.090
appears in every Pixar movie.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.049
He's got an even better record for appearances
than the storied Pizza Planet truck.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:51.820
Since his first appearance as Hamm the piggy
bank in Toy Story, Ratzenberger has been a

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.240
sort of good luck charm for Pixar.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.399
In The Incredibles, he lent his voice to the
villainous Underminer, who drilled up from

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:00.899
beneath the earth's surface at the end of
the film.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:05.399
Since Incredibles 2 picks up exactly where
the first left off, Ratzenberger remains in

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:07.060
the mix for the second round.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.310
He lives to fight another day, too, which
unexpectedly makes the Underminer Incredibles

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.670
2's biggest sequel hook.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.100
Seeing Underminer near the end of the credits'
stylized summary of the movie's events reminds

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.560
us that he's still at large.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.580
So if an Incredibles 3 does happen, it's a
good bet that the Underminer will be back

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.230
for another showdown.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.230
A113

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:32.760
One of the most famous Pixar Easter eggs is
also one of the easiest to miss.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.070
"A113" is a short sequence of letters and
numbers that does not, on its own, mean anything.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.350
So why has it appeared in so many movies?

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.530
The sequence refers to a specific classroom
at the California Institute of the Arts, where

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.900
many influential animators and filmmakers
attended college.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.840
It's not just a Pixar reference, either, A113
has also appeared in movies and TV shows from

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.180
Family Guy to The Hunger Games' sequel.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.410
Since the Easter egg is so inconspicuous,
it pops up a lot throughout Incredibles 2.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:04.430
It appears on the side of a runaway train,
shows up on a license plate, and turns up

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.070
on the theater marquee at the end of the movie,
and that's just a few examples.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.070
It's fitting that the reference would show
up In Incredibles 2, considering that writer-director

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:17.090
Brad Bird is the one who started the tradition
over 30 years ago in an episode of the show

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.270
Amazing Stories.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.480
Safari Court

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.820
After an exploding jet falls on their home
during the climax of The Incredibles, the

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.410
sequel sees the family living in a throwback-style
motel called Safari Court.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.900
The distinctive, familiar-looking building
is recognizable for a reason: It's based on

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.270
a real place.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:40.010
The Parrs' temporary residence is clearly
modeled after the Safari Inn, a semi-famous

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:44.610
hotel in Burbank, California, located only
a few streets away from Walt Disney Studios.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.860
Clearly, the photogenic location made an impression
on the filmmakers, and they wouldn't be the

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.230
first ones to use it.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.340
The inn has hosted scenes in popular movies
and a ton of TV shows.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.700
Its weird ubiquity in pop culture makes it
the landmark equivalent of a familiar face,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.550
beautifully recreated in Incredibles 2.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.480
Evelyn Deavor's evil endeavors

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.150
Incredibles 2 doesn't try all that hard to
hide the fact that the brilliant Evelyn Deavor

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.390
is actually the movie's true villain.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.080
One of the most telling hints as to her true
nature?

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.540
That would be her name.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.069
Instead of reading her name as "Eve-a-Lynn",
pronounce her name as "EVE-ill" Say it all

00:04:19.069 --> 00:04:23.460
at once, and it sounds like "evil endeavor",
which pretty much says it all, right?

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.370
In any case, it's enough evidence to let an
engaged viewer see the plot turn coming from

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.639
miles away.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:32.550
The real twist, by comparison, is that Evelyn's
brother Winston actually turns out to be a

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.270
pretty solid guy.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.740
New math for life

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.840
Bob's difficulty in helping Dash with his
math homework is one of the funniest gags

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.800
in Incredibles 2, and it hits the hardest
for parents around Bob's age.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.110
In case you haven't been around an intermediate
school math class for awhile, you may be surprised

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.300
to learn that they really did change math,
whoever "they" are.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.419
"I don't know that way.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:54.621
Why would they change math?

00:04:54.621 --> 00:04:57.440
Math is math!"

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.080
It's not just Bob being clueless.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.090
Dash's "New Math for Life" textbook and its
indecipherable problem-solving methods are

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:08.289
a reference to the often-confusing Common
Core method of learning and doing arithmetic.

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:12.310
The competing old and new approaches have
been fueling debates among parents and teachers

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.840
for years, so if you hear some grown-ups laughing
particularly loudly at the movie's homework

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.080
sequence, the Common Core callout is probably
what got them.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.120
Thanks for watching!

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.740
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.190
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

